{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and he inherited his title after his father suffered assassination by the Hashshashin order. His early reign involved significant conflict with the Knights Hospitaller regarding territorial disputes yet he eventually secured his position through diplomatic marriages and military alliances. Raymond married Eschiva of Bures which granted him control over the Principality of Galilee and this union made him one of the most powerful barons in the entire kingdom. His political influence grew substantially because he acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance.\n\n*Early Life and Succession Challenges*\nThe young count faced immediate threats upon assuming power since his father died violently and left the county vulnerable to external attacks. He managed to stabilize the region by forging peace treaties with nearby Muslim rulers while also defending his lands against occasional raids. His nobility recognized his capability despite his youth and they supported his claim against rival factions that sought to undermine his authority. This period established his reputation as a shrewd politician who preferred negotiation over unnecessary warfare whenever possible.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nRaymond assumed the regency for King Baldwin IV and this role placed him at the center of intense court intrigue involving the Lusignan family. He opposed the marriage of Sibylla to Guy of Lusignan because he feared their combined power would destabilize the realm and lead to disaster. His faction known as the pullani advocated for peaceful coexistence with Saladin whereas the court party pushed for aggressive military action. These internal divisions weakened the Christian states significantly before the crucial Battle of Hattin took place in 1187.\n\n*Final Years and Legacy*\nThe count retired to Tripoli after the catastrophic defeat at Hattin where many Christian leaders were captured or killed including Guy of Lusignan. Raymond died shortly after these events possibly from pleurisy or natural causes related to stress and age. His legacy remains complex because historians debate whether his caution saved the kingdom temporarily or if his opposition to Guy hastened its collapse. He left no legitimate heirs so his counties passed to distant relatives and his death marked the end of an era for the Crusader states in the Levant. The loss of his diplomatic skill proved detrimental as the kingdom soon lost Jerusalem to Saladin forces.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -3.576285e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0012605384}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0027649445}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.07889876}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.00011099001}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.6689314e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.02656e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0015340287}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.05897383}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}